"குவை","parts":[{"text":"குவை","lemma":"குவை","gloss":"thick"}],"gloss":"thick"},{"surface":"இரும்","parts":[{"text":"இரும்","lemma":"இரும்","gloss":"dark"}],"gloss":"dark"},{"surface":"கூந்தல்","parts":[{"text":"கூந்தல்","lemma":"கூந்தல்","gloss":"hair"}],"gloss":"hair"},{"surface":"கொய்தல்","parts":[{"text":"கொய்","lemma":"கொய்","gloss":"cut"},{"text":"தல்","lemma":"தல்","gloss":"verbal noun suffix"}],"gloss":"cutting"},{"surface":"கண்டே","parts":[{"text":"கண்டு","lemma":"காண்","gloss":"having seen"},{"text":"ஏ","lemma":"ஏ","gloss":"clitic"}],"gloss":"indeed seeing"}]}],"literal":"As the stars-shining sky's spreading darkness departs, / following the swift movement's nature, without failing its own course, / the strong anger-intensified fear-inspiring sun, / along with the moonlight-shining moon, as if joined to the earth, / the battling, difficult-to-oppose strength's allied kings, / on the affliction-filled battlefield, making them wither, / at the time you captured their bound drum, / deviating from its position, when struck, its strong joints shaking, / did your spear escape from shattering, O Sezhiyan, / destroying them so that their breasts-shining chests burned with grief, / with limitless lamentation as they forgot their bodies and perished, / as the widowhood of the bright-foreheaded women increased, / resembling shining black sand, beautiful and soft, / seeing the cutting of their thick, dark hair?","translation":"O Sezhiyan! When you crushed the allied kings of formidable strength on the terrifying battlefield, making them wither, and captured their war-drums, did your spear escape from shattering? For, when it struck them, its strong joints shook and deviated from their position. You destroyed them so that their chests, once adorned by beautiful breasts, burned with grief. Seeing the bright-foreheaded women, forgotten of themselves in limitless lamentation for their fallen husbands, cut off their thick, dark, soft, and beautiful hair that resembled shining black sand as their widowhood increased, did your spear escape being broken?","modern":"விண்மீன்கள் ஒளிரும் வானத்தின் பரந்த இருள் விலக, தன் இயல்பான வழியிலிருந்து மாறாமல் விரைந்து செல்லும் தன்மையைக் கொண்ட அச்சம் தரும் கதிரவன், நிலவொளி வீசும் திங்களோடு நிலத்தில் வந்து சேர்ந்தாற்போல, பகைமை கொண்டு ஒன்றுபட்ட வலிமை மிக்க வேந்தர்களைத் துன்பம் நிறைந்த போர்க்களத்தில் அழியச் செய்து, அவர்களின் போர் முரசினைக் கைப்பற்றியபோது, உன் வேல் சிதையாமல் தப்பியதோ செழியனே! மார்புகள் வருந்தும்படி அவர்களை அழித்து, தம் கணவரை இழந்து மெய்ம்மறந்து எல்லையற்ற துயரத்தோடு அழும் ஒளிரும் நெற்றியையுடைய மகளிர், ஒளிரும் கருமணல் போன்ற அழகிய மென்மையான அடர்ந்த கரிய கூந்தலைக் கொய்து கைம்மை நோன்பு ஏற்பதைக் கண்டும் உன் வேல் உடையாமல் தப்பியதோ!","explanation":"The poet Kalladanar addresses the Pandyan king Nedunchezhiyan, who won the great battle of Talaiyalanganam. The poet praises the king's absolute victory over the allied kings who opposed him. He compares the allied kings falling together to the sun and the moon falling to the earth at once. The poem vividly describes the aftermath of the battle: the enemy kings are slain, and their wives, in deep grief, cut off their beautiful, dark, soft hair that resembles shining black sand. The poet asks rhetorically if the king's spear escaped being shattered after such a fierce battle, highlighting the intensity of the combat and the absolute destruction of the enemy.","model":"gemini-3.5-flash"}}